{
  "schema_version": 2,
  "title": "ASCII characters and code table",
  "summary": "Look up the binary representation by decimal code or character, and distinguish control characters from printable characters.",
  "context": [
    "ASCII uses 7 bits to represent 128 characters, with codes ranging from 0 to 127. The figure displays binary values as 8-bit single bytes grouped into nibbles of 4 bits, with the highest bit fixed at 0; the table below merges the figure's four column panels into a single complete table ordered by increasing decimal code."
  ],
  "description": [
    "By code range: 0 to 31 and 127 are control characters, where 127 is DEL for delete (left blank in the figure's meaning column); 32 is the space character SP; 48 to 57 are the digits 0 to 9; 65 to 90 are uppercase letters A to Z; and 97 to 122 are lowercase letters a to z. Printable punctuation and symbols occupy 33 to 47, 58 to 64, 91 to 96, and 123 to 126.",
    "The table’s meaning column uses the labels in the English figure. Code 14, SO (Shift out), selects an alternate character set; code 15, SI (Shift in), switches back to the original set. Code 27, ESC (Escape), can introduce an escape sequence. Codes 10 (LF, Line feed), 12 (FF, Form feed), and 13 (CR, Carriage return) control line breaks, page breaks, and returning to the start of a line, respectively.",
    "A character glyph differs from its numeric code: the digit character 0 has code 48 (binary 0011 0000), whereas code 0 is the null character NUL (binary 0000 0000); both also differ from the space character SP (32) and the delete character DEL (127)."
  ],
  "frames": [
    {
      "id": "source-43",
      "book_path": "chapter_data_structure/character_encoding.assets/ascii_table.png",
      "title": "ASCII characters and code table",
      "explanation": "For the digit characters 0 to 9, the upper four bits are always 0011 and the lower four bits run from 0000 to 1001. Uppercase and lowercase forms of the same letter differ by 32, changing only the 6th bit from the right (weight 32): A is 65 (binary 0100 0001) and a is 97 (binary 0110 0001). For instance, to look up a newline, locate LF at decimal row 10 in the table below."
    }
  ],
